# Cline Project Rules

## Repository Overview

This repository uses two separate Git worktrees.

Main development worktree
Location:
/workspaces/fhir-agent

Purpose:
- Application source code
- Feature branches
- Pull Requests
- Code reviews

AI archive worktree
Location:
/workspaces/.ai-chat-history

Branch:
ai-chat-history

Purpose:
- AI conversation archive
- Export scripts
- Archive tooling
- Human-readable conversation history

Treat these as two independent development environments.

---

## Terminal Execution

- Execute only one logical shell operation per execute_command call.
- Never combine inspection, modification, testing, committing and pushing into one shell command.
- Avoid chaining commands with && or semicolons.
- Avoid interactive commands.
- Use non-interactive Git flags whenever possible.
- After every execute_command, inspect the result before continuing.
- If the UI marks any command as "Skipped", stop immediately.
- Never assume a skipped command executed successfully.
- Report the skipped command and wait for me to reload Cline.

---

## Git Rules — Application Code

Applies only to:

/workspaces/fhir-agent

- Never commit directly to main.
- Always work on a feature branch.
- Push the branch to origin.
- Never merge yourself.
- Create or update a Pull Request when requested.
- Summarise files changed and tests executed before asking for review.

---

## Git Rules — AI Archive

Applies only to:

/workspaces/.ai-chat-history

- Never create a Pull Request.
- Never merge ai-chat-history into main.
- Never ask me to review archive-tooling changes through GitHub PRs.
- Commit directly to the ai-chat-history branch after testing.
- Push directly to origin/ai-chat-history.
- Archive commits should remain isolated from application development.

---

## Testing

- Run tests before proposing a commit.
- Never claim tests passed unless they were executed.
- Report failures immediately.

---

## Safety

- Ask before destructive operations.
- Never delete files unless requested.
- Never overwrite important files without understanding their purpose.

---

## Large Tasks

- Break large work into small milestones.
- Report progress after each milestone.
- Stop if confidence becomes low.
- Ask for clarification rather than guessing.

---

## Communication

- Briefly explain your implementation plan before making major changes.
- When finished, provide:
  - files changed
  - tests run
  - commit hashes
  - branch name
  - any limitations

## Repository Awareness

Before executing any Git command:

- Determine which worktree is active.
- Confirm which branch is currently checked out.
- State which repository and branch will be modified.
- Refuse to run Git commands if the intended worktree is ambiguous.  